# ============================================================================
# P0-LAUNCH-01 — line-ending policy.
#
# Until this file existed the repository had NO checkout protection at all.
#
# Every text blob here is committed LF — verified across all tracked files at
# 85511ce: zero non-binary blobs contain CR. Nothing enforced that on the way
# OUT, and Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` as its default, so a
# permitted, entirely ordinary clone materialises those LF blobs as CRLF.
#
# That contamination is invisible to `git status`: `autocrlf` normalises CRLF
# back to LF on read, so the worktree compares clean against the LF blob while
# every file on disk carries CR. Nothing in the repository noticed.
#
# It is not cosmetic. This repository gates itself largely by INSPECTING ITS
# OWN SOURCE TEXT, and those regexes are anchored with `\n` / `$`. In
# JavaScript `.` does not match `\r` (a line terminator) and `$` without the
# `m` flag matches only end-of-input, so a `\r` silently defeats them. On a
# CRLF materialisation of these exact blobs, 13 tests fail and the
# `check:ci-workflow-integrity` governance gate reports a FALSE failure — its
# `stripComments` helper stops stripping, and prose that literally reads
# "no `|| true`" trips the assertion forbidding `|| true`.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the checkout deterministic on every platform and every
# `core.autocrlf` setting. The blobs are unchanged by this file — they were
# already LF; this only fixes how they are written to disk.
#
# tests/line-ending-policy.test.mjs is the negative guard: it fails if this
# policy is weakened or if any tracked text file is ever materialised with CR.
# ============================================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary artifacts — never pass through a text filter.
#
# `text=auto` already detects these by content, but the frozen upstream
# tarballs are CHECKSUM-VERIFIED: check:tarball-purity and
# check:packaged-aoc-artifacts pin every vendored artifact by SHA-256, reading
# the identities from vendor/aoc-consumer.lock.json. Their integrity must not
# rest on a heuristic, so it is declared here rather than inferred.
#
# This comment deliberately names no version. It previously spelled out
# @aoc/protocol 0.2.0-rc.0 and @aoc-enterprise/runtime 1.2.0, and went stale
# when P0-PKG-09 repinned both — the same duplicated-identity defect that made
# those two gates stale, and which was fixed there by making them lock-driven.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.tgz  binary
*.png  binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
